      "id": 1,
      "text": "Silver Lake Reservoir was named after Herman Silver, the 19th‑century water commissioner who secured its construction in 1907.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Lake%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The upper basin of the reservoir was originally called Ivanhoe, honoring Walter Scott’s 1819 novel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Lake%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the 1920s and 1930s, artists, writers, actors, and musicians flocked to Silver Lake, turning it into a bohemian enclave.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Lake%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Tom Mix’s Western film studio, Mixville, once stood on Glendale Boulevard, making Silver Lake an early Hollywood hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Lake%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s streets were laid out in the 1920s following hill contours, creating the distinctive Mixville blocks that now host shops and a public library.",
